==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|emblématique}}. See {{m|en|emblem}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɛmbləˈmatɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-emblematic.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{enPR|ĕmb-lə-mă-tĭk}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-emblematic.oga|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|em|blem|at|ic}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Serving as, or relating to a [[symbol]], [[emblem]] or [[illustration]] of a [[type]].
#: {{syn|en|symbolic|illustrative}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=149|volume=II|text=All was dreary—all was '''emblematic''' of that change and barrenness which passes away from nature, but never from the heart;...}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[typical|Typical]].
#: {{syn|en|representative|exemplary|characteristic|iconic}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Women’s World Cup 2015: England beaten by France in Group F opener|work={{w|The Guardian}} (London)|date=9 June 2015|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/09/france-england-womens-world-cup-group-f-match-report|passage=With Le Sommer withdrawn, France – now with five in midfield – seemed content to hold on for a deceptively narrow win. Such containment tactics can prove high risk but the only threat England could muster was a shot from Aluko which flew high and wide. It seemed entirely '''emblematic''' of their afternoon.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2019-03-13|author=Oliver Norgrove|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2019/mar/13/cake-unicorns-brexit-zealots|title=Hypnotised by cake and unicorns, the Brexit perfectionists have blown it|work=The Guardian|passage=The result of yesterday’s meaningful vote in the House of Commons, much like the first in January, was '''emblematic''' of a very striking Brexit reality: that the principle barrier to leaving the EU comes from the very people most desperate to see it happen.}}

